/** Retrofit batch AZ -- upgrading old 2-case gpe-history problems to the 4-case standard (Sample +
 * boundary + tricky + stress). All solvers below were independently written and verified byte-for-
 * byte against each problem's real official Sample before being trusted to generate new tricky/
 * stress expected output.
 *
 * gpe-22351-quirksome-squares: instead of brute-forcing every d-digit number, iterate the candidate
 *   HALF-SUM s from 0 to 2*(10^(d/2)-1), compute s^2, and check whether splitting its d-digit
 *   zero-padded representation in half sums back to s -- O(10^(d/2)) instead of O(10^d).
 * gpe-22821-oil-deposits: standard 8-directional flood fill counting connected '@' components.
 * gpe-23571-smith-numbers: search upward from n+1, testing each candidate via O(sqrt(n)) trial-division
 *   factorization (digit-sum of the number vs. digit-sum of its prime factors with multiplicity,
 *   excluding primes themselves per the problem's definition).
 * gpe-23581-glass-beads: Booth's O(n) algorithm for the lexicographically smallest string rotation,
 *   which naturally returns the smallest starting index among ties (verified against the already-
 *   seeded boundary case's all-identical-character edge case).
 * gpe-23651-the-jackpot: Kadane's O(n) maximum-subarray-sum algorithm; "Losing streak." whenever the
 *   best achievable sum is <= 0.
 * gpe-23661-bit-mask: bit-DP over the 32 bits from high to low, tracking tight-to-L/tight-to-U flags
 *   (memoized on (bit, tightLow, tightHigh), independent of N, L, U's actual values, so at most
 *   32*2*2=128 states), maximizing N|M first and, among ties, minimizing M. Verified against brute
 *   force for small ranges and at the full 32-bit unsigned upper bound.
 * gpe-23671-camel-trading: O(n^3) interval DP tracking both the max and min achievable value for every
 *   subexpression (necessary because minimizing an outer product may require maximizing one of its
 *   factors when the other is negative -- not an issue here since all values are positive, but the
 *   standard max/min-both technique is required regardless since a max subexpression combined via '*'
 *   doesn't always yield the max of the whole).
 */
